{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the serpent was cunning, etc.</b> One needs to know for what reason the verse informed us that the serpent was cunning?" "<b>Further,</b> I set my heart to search out the cunning which he employed in the matter, and I saw nothing but an act of wickedness and wondrous wisdom on his part." "<b>Now,</b> the matter will be explained upon understanding the intent of his words in his saying <b>although He said</b>, etc., for his words appear like words of folly; therefore the verse preceded by informing us that the serpent was cunning, in order to scrutinize his sayings, through which he managed to seduce and lead astray the woman who feared HaShem." "<b>And behold,</b> in the order of his words he intended to express three ideas, and through them he achieved his intent." "First, he came to inform her of one piece of knowledge: that since she had not managed to eat of the tree of knowledge, the matter would be in her eyes as though she were not eating of any tree of the garden. The intent in this is that all of them are as nothing compared to it, and they were as though they were nothing, given the greatness of its stature and importance, etc. And this is his saying <b>although He said</b>, etc. — the meaning being: it should be in your eyes as though you were commanded concerning all of them. By this he intended to magnify her desire for it and to render worthless all her cravings for all the trees of the garden. And this is the trait which the evil inclination practices with those who heed it: it removes from them desire for what is permitted and makes it repugnant in their eyes, and magnifies in their eyes desire for what is forbidden beyond compare." "(2) It directs itself to another order of seduction, namely that the one who instructs her is the serpent, and it is on the way of what our Sages of blessed memory said (Genesis 18) that the Tree of Knowledge is what HaShem planted first in the Garden, and from it He grafted all the trees of the Garden. Therefore the seducer came and said that even though HaShem forbade only the Tree of Knowledge, it must follow that all the other trees of the Garden are likewise forbidden, for the law of grafting applies to them, as we learned in chapter 1 of tractate Orlah: if one layered it year after year, one counts for it from the year that was severed — thus far. And the intention in this is that before the severing, even though they are rooted in the earth, nevertheless their primary nourishment is drawn from that [original] tree and they were like branches of the old tree, and so Rambam and Rash explained. And this is what the seducer argued in saying <b>af ki amar</b> — etc., meaning that if she were to accept upon herself to fulfill this thing, she would need to separate herself from everything, because all the trees of the Garden still draw nourishment from the Tree of Knowledge — so he claimed — for they are grafted and not yet severed, for only a short time had elapsed from when HaShem planted the Garden until the time of His commandment concerning it." "(3) It directs itself in His saying <b>af</b> — etc., to the effect that Chavah was not commanded by the mouth of the Creator but by the mouth of a man, as we explained in its place. Therefore the seducer came and told her one piece of information, namely that HaShem's commandment upon her was that she not eat of any tree of the Garden, for he is a witness to the matter that HaShem so commanded her. And the seducer's intention in this involved two designs for evil: the first, that she would believe his words and forbid everything, and then he could enter with the argument of rendering the impossible, and he would have an opening to enter through it to seduce her. And the second, he intended that the testimony of the man who commanded her concerning the Tree of Knowledge alone should become discredited testimony — and this is the reasoning of Beit Shammai in chapter 4 of tractate Eduyot: one regarding whom two sets of witnesses testified, these testifying that he took two vows and these testifying that he took three — Beit Shammai say the testimony is divided and there is no nazirite status, and Beit Hillel say that the two are included in the three — thus far. And in Sanhedrin (31a) they established the dispute between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel as one set, meaning, one of the two said two and one said three, etc. From this the seducer reasoned: since there were only two — Adam said He forbade only the Tree of Knowledge, and the seducer said He forbade everything — the testimony is divided. And after I wrote this I found one statement in Tikkunei haZohar, Tikkun 59, that agrees with our explanation, as it wrote, and this is its language: וחויא בישא אעבר הכא על לא תענה ברעך עד שקר דסהיד שקרא שאמר ״אף כי אמר אלהים״ — etc., thus far. And this is his nature: to lie and to breed in a person's heart false beliefs and opinions, may the Merciful One save us. And at times he intends to exaggerate and magnify transgressions in a person's heart, and then he will turn and say to him that it is among the things impossible to grasp — and there is none more cunning in the world than this." "<b>And the reason</b> that HaShem did so is that the entire design of HaShem was for the sake of attaining the wondrous good, and it depends upon this mode of service. Therefore He magnified the dimension of cunning and its power, and according to the suffering that a person will endure and the wisdom he will acquire to escape it, so will he ascend in degrees." "<b>And he said to the woman</b> — etc. Meaning: through the chirping that non-speaking animals speak in, and at that time they understood all chirpings, etc., and even the speech of the inanimate. For everything that HaShem created has, according to its level, a mode of speech for the purpose of praising its Maker, as it says (Proverbs 16:4): ״כל פעל ה למענהו״, and go out and learn from Perek Shirah. And we find among the great ones of Israel those who understood every chirp and utterance, as they said of him of blessed memory (Sukkah 28b): they said about Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai, upon him be peace, etc. — and all the more so Adam the first, the formation of the hands of the Blessed One."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:38:37.351306971Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}